    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_078a35eb0432c746d8f1a457208cbbcf43e338f25a7f31e03c9a5ec9ee0b9341",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The proof of Theorem 1 is factored through two regimes of an auxiliary estimate: Lemma 1 gives an ordinary bound for a real nonprincipal character and a strengthened bound conditional on a real zero, while Lemma 2 is declared to supply the needed estimates for the auxiliary divisor-sum g(n). This re-represents the immediate proof obligation as control of one coefficient sum under an added zero hypothesis, making the conditional strengthening explicit and modular. It does not show why g(n) has the needed properties, derive either lemma, or explain the later zero-repulsion composition; those burdens remain outside the span. The assumptions that the character is real and nonprincipal, and that the stronger branch has an actual real zero, are essential.",
