    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_4d06613efb50a9c0085e707032d6ca8fd8e78a8a1e4cfe3de177843543f96842",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The proof constrains the same weighted sum from two sides. Its coefficient representation supplies a positive lower bound; assuming two zeros or a nonsimple zero in the stated interval supplies an intermediate parameter with L(1-t) nonpositive and L'(1-t) zero. In Lemma 3's analytic representation, those conditions suppress or control the main terms, while the remaining errors must be small enough to give an upper bound incompatible with the coefficient-side lower bound. Thus a global zero configuration is compressed into local value-and-derivative data for a shared observable. The mechanism gains a contradiction from representation change, but it is quantitative rather than merely an opposite-sign argument: its validity still depends on the precise interval, lower bound, error control, positivity of L(1), and parameter restrictions, all formula-level details that the OCR does not preserve safely.",
