    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_dce88a10045b7ad0dc7cd5a0cbaa759567e17a16a31de80ff21edbb3b850a620",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The two zero hypotheses are coupled through the coefficient expansion involving the two original characters and their nonprincipal product, not by assigning both zeros to the product L-function. The ordinary estimate (2.1) is licensed for the nonprincipal product character, while the zero-sensitive estimate (2.2) is applied to each original character carrying its assumed zero. Combining these differently assigned bounds into one positive sum and then choosing the auxiliary real parameter converts separate constraints into a repulsion conclusion. This gains a common comparison for the two characters but loses the individual provenance of terms after aggregation; the proof still depends on nonprincipality of the product, the original zero hypotheses, positivity of the expanded sum, and the quantitative optimization in the OCR-damaged inequalities.",
